From c7c965560e79514e887dc06dfcca2e6eb914365c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch <patch@local>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 00:31:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add view-only mode, reachable from the login screen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A button on the login screen starts a session that can read everything
and write nothing: the map, the gallery, the photos, the comment
threads and the reaction tallies, with no upload, no comment, no
reaction, no star, no pin and no delete.

What makes it view-only is the server, not the interface. Every route
that writes now calls requireMember instead of requireUser, and
requireMember rejects a guest with a 403 through the existing guard.
Hiding buttons only stops the app from offering a write, not from
accepting one -- a guest holds a valid session cookie and can send any
request by hand. All eleven write routes were checked against a live
server with a guest cookie; all eleven refuse.

  photos POST · photos/check POST · photos/location PATCH
  photos/[id] PATCH + DELETE · reaction PUT · favorite PUT
  comments POST · comments/[id] DELETE · places POST
  uploads/presign POST

403 rather than 401, because the session is fine -- it just isn't
allowed to do this. A 401 would bounce a perfectly valid guest to the
login screen.

Guests share one identity, uid "guest". It is never written to any
table, and in the two reads scoped to "mine" it simply matches nothing:
no favorites, no myReaction. Five characters can't collide with a
nanoid, so it can't borrow a member's rows either. A token with no role
claim is read as a member, since sessions last 60 days and every
existing one belongs to a member; and a guest token is never admin,
whatever the claim says.

The UI half is a ViewerProvider in the app layout, reading the role off
the cookie the server just verified rather than fetching it, so nothing
flashes a write button before hiding it. It defaults to canWrite: false
-- a missing provider then hides buttons from a member, which is
visible immediately, instead of showing buttons to a guest, which
isn't. Reaction bars become plain tallies, comment threads keep the
conversation and lose the composer, and the star, re-pin, delete,
date-edit, upload, bulk-place and merge affordances go. /upload
redirects guests in the middleware and again in the page.

/login no longer bounces a signed-in guest home: signing in properly is
how a view-only session gets upgraded, and bouncing them would make the
form unreachable without first finding a sign-out button. The "View
only" badge in the top bar links there.

Worth being explicit about the tradeoff, since it's the whole point of
the feature: this makes the trip's photos, and their locations,
readable by anyone who has the address. No password, no invitation, no
audit trail. If that's more than intended, gate the route on an env var
-- one condition in /api/auth/guest and one in LoginForm -- so the door
can be closed without a redeploy of anything else.
